       72  '''Iringa Region''' ({{langx|sw|Mkoa wa Iringa}}) is one of [[Tanzania]]'s 31 [[Regions of Tanzania|administrative regions]]. The region covers an area of {{cvt|35,503|km2}}. {{Cite web|url=https://iringa.go.tz/|title=Iringa Region Size}} The region is comparable in size to the combined land area of the nation state of [[Guinea Bissau]]. {{Convert|36,125|km2|abbr=on}} for Guinea Bissau at {{cite w
       73  ==Geography==
       74  The [[elevation]] of Iringa varies from 900 to 2,300 meters above sea level. A significant scarp that can reach 800 meters in height and is the eastern portion of the [[Great Rift Valley]] surrounds the area on all sides. Iringa is hence situated in Tanzania's southern highlands, bordering Mbeya, Njombe, Morogoro, Dodoma, and Singida areas. {{Cite web|url=https://www.fsdt.or.tz/wp-content/uploads/
       75  The region is drained by the Little Ruaha and the [[Great Ruaha River|Great Ruaha]] rivers. The lake created by the [[Mtera Dam]] is the other significant water body here. {{cite web|title=Iringa Region socio-economic profile, 2013|url=http://www.nbs.go.tz/nbs/takwimu/SocialEconomicProfiles/IringaRegionalProfile%202013.zip|date=2013|access-date=October 17, 2016|publisher=National Bureau of Statist
       76  The region can be divided into three zones - highland, midland and lowland. The highland zone is towards the east of the region. This area experiences a rainy season between November and May with annual precipitation ranging from {{convert|500|-|1500|mm|in|abbr=on}}. The period between June and September is cold and dry. The midland zone, in the central part of the region, lies at a height of {{co
       77  About 16% of the land in Iringa Region is forested. The region is host to the [[Ruaha National Park]], famous for its large herd of elephants and over 400 species of birds. Other animals include lions, [[sable antelope]]s and [[kudu]]. A second park, [[Udzungwa Mountains National Park]] in Iringa Rural District, is less visited. {{Cite book|title=Focus on Tanzania|chapter=Focus: Iringa Region|year
       78  ==Demographics==
       79  [[File:The National Archives UK - CO 1069-164-18.jpg|thumb|Hehe Warrior]]
       80  [[File:The National Archives UK - CO 1069-159-91.jpg|thumb|Adam Hapi, former Hehe King in the 1960s]]
       81  Iringa Region has a total population of 1,192,728. {{cite web|page=103|title=2012 population and housing census: population distribution by administrative areas|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130612082906/http://www.nbs.go.tz/sensa/PDF/Census%20General%20Report%20-%2029%20March%202013_Combined_Final%20for%20Printing.pdf|archive-date=June 12, 2013|publisher=National Bureau of Statistics|
       82  [[Hehe people]] are the largest ethnic group living in the region. Other major populations are those of [[Bena people|Bena]] and [[Kinga people|Kinga]] groups. Pangwa, [[Chaga people|Chaga]], [[Nyakyusa people|Nyakyusa]] and [[Ngoni people|Ngoni]] can be found in urban areas primarily engaged in business in the region. other immigrants into Iringa include [[Maasai people|Maasai]] and [[sukuma peop
       83  ==Administrative divisions==
       84  ===Districts===
       85  Iringa Region is divided into three [[Districts of Tanzania|districts]] ([[Iringa]], Kilolo and Mufindi), each administered by a council. The districts have local authorities operating under their supervision. These are three district councils, one municipal council, and one town council as listed in the table:
