      111  ==Politics==
      112  Iringa Region elects seven representatives to the [[National Assembly of Tanzania]]. In the [[Tanzanian general election, 2015|2015 general election]], six candidates from the ruling [[Chama Cha Mapinduzi]] and one from [[CHADEMA]] won their respective seats. {{cite web|title=Wabunge wa Kuchaguliwa 2015-2020|page=3|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151208070605/http://www.nec.go.tz/uploads
      113  ==Economy==
      114  Iringa Region has the fifth largest GDP out of the 30 regions in Tanzania. On a per-capita basis, Iringa's 2012 figure of about TSh 1,400,000 ranks it second only to [[Dar es Salaam Region]] which includes the capital of Tanzania. {{cite book|title=Tanzania Human Development Report 2014|year=2015|page=9|publisher=[[UNDP]]|access-date=October 17, 2016|isbn=978-9987-770-00-7|url=http://hdr.undp.org/
      115  Agriculture is the mainstay of Iringa's economy accounting for 85% of its GDP. Between 2008 and 2011, an average {{convert|345,000|ha|mi2}} of land was planted with food crops annually. Maize is the dominant cereal with about {{convert|245,000|ha|mi2}} of land devoted to it. Beans are second most important food crop being grown on {{convert|56,000|ha|mi2}}. Cash crops take about 56,000.00 hectares
      116  The industry in Iringa Region is mostly small scaled and largely located in the [[Iringa]] municipality. The food industry consists of tomatoes and chili processing, milk processing, grain milling. There is also carpentry and oil processing which comprise the bulk of industrial units found within the Iringa Region.
